===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# Including or involving every part or member of a given or implied entity, whole, etc.; [[common]] to all, [[universal]]. {{defdate|from 13th c.}}
#: {{syn|en|common|universal|Thesaurus:generic}}
#: {{ant|en|specific|special|particular|Thesaurus:specific}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=c. 1495|author=w:John Skelton|title="Vppon a deedman's hed"|passage=It is '''generall''' / To be mortall: / I haue well espyde / No man may hym hyde / From Deth holow eyed {{...}}.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1842|author=Douglas Jerrold|chapter=Mr Peppersorn ‘At Home’|title=Cakes and Ale|passage="Among us!" was the '''general''' shout, and Peppersorn sat frozen to his chair.}}
#* {{RQ:Tennyson In Memoriam|XLVI|69|That each, who seems a separate whole,<br>Should move his rounds, and fusing all<br>The skirts of self again, should fall<br>Remerging in the '''general''' Soul,<br>Is faith as vague as all unsweet: {{...}}}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1946|author=w:Bertrand Russell|title=History of Western Philosophy|others=book 1, part 3|chapter=Stoicism|passage=Undoubtedly the age of the Antonines was much better than any later age until the Renaissance, from the point of view of the '''general''' happiness.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2006-10-15|author=Ruth Sutherland|title=Invite public to the private equity party|journal=The Observer|passage=One advantage of having profitable companies in Britain is that they pay large sums in corporate tax into the Exchequer, which in theory at least is used for the '''general''' good.}}
# {{lb|en|sometimes|_|postpositive}} {{ng|Applied to a person (as a postmodifier or a normal preceding adjective) to indicate [[supreme]] [[rank]], in civil or military titles, and later in other terms}}; [[pre-eminent]]. {{defdate|from 14th c.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1865|author=Edward Cust|title=Lives of the Warriors of the Thirty Years War|page=527|passage=For these successes he obtained the rank of Field-Marshal '''General'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2002|author=James Turner|title=Libertines and Radicals in Early Modern London|page=122|passage=He becomes the chief chartered libertine, the whoremaster-'''general''' flourishing his "standard" over a female army {{...}}.}}
# [[prevalent|Prevalent]] or [[widespread]] among a given class or area; [[common]], [[usual]]. {{defdate|from 14th c.}}
#: {{syn|en|typical|usual|common|Thesaurus:common}}
#: {{ant|en|abnormal|uncommon}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1817|author=[[w:Walter Scott|Sir Walter Scott]]|title=Rob Roy|chapter=IX|passage=‘I can't quite afford you the sympathy you expect upon this score,’ I replied; ‘the misfortune is so '''general''', that it belongs to one half of the species {{...}}.’}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2008-12-20|author=John Patterson|title=Home movies|journal=The Guardian|passage=The '''general''' opinion on Baz Luhrmann's overstuffed epic ''Australia'' seems to be that it throws in everything but the kitchen sink, and then tosses that in too, just to be sure.}}
# Not limited in use or application; applicable across a broad range. {{defdate|from 14th c.}}
#: {{syn|en|broad|generic|Thesaurus:generic}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=1924-03-17|journal=Time|passage=M. Venizelos went to Athens from Paris early last January in response to a '''general''' invitation from the Greek populace.}}
#* {{RQ:Time|date=1947-10-20|title=Russian Catechism|passage=Already in the primary school work is conducted for the purpose of equipping the pupils with those elements of '''general''' knowledge which are closely related to the military preparation of future warriors.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=2009|author=Douglas P. Zipes|journal=Saturday Evening Post|volume=281|number=1|page=20|passage=Supraventricular tachycardia (SVT) is a '''general''' term indicating a rapid heartbeat (tachycardia) coming from the top chambers of the heart - in essence, above (supra) the lower chamber (ventricular).}}
# Giving or consisting of only the most important aspects of something, ignoring minor details; [[indefinite]]. {{defdate|from 16th c.}}
#: {{syn|en|impressionistic}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1817|author=[[w:Walter Scott|Sir Walter Scott]]|title=Rob Roy|chapter=X|passage=As she thus spoke, the entrance of the servants with dinner cut off all conversation but that of a '''general''' nature.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1941|author=w:W Somerset Maugham|title=Up at the Villa|publisher=Vintage|year_published=2004|page=24|passage=There was a moment's pause. The Princess broke in with some casual remark and once more the conversation became '''general'''.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2006-07-16|author=Kevin Nance|title=Ghosts of the White City|journal=Chicago Sun-Times|passage=The quick answer is that the 1893 Exposition was simply so important — "the greatest event in the history of the country since the Civil War," as Harper's put it that October — but that feels too '''general'''.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=2008|author=Robert P. Maloney|title=The Quiet Carpenter|journal=America|volume=199|number=19|page=18|passage=Given the scarcity of relevant historical detail in the New Testament, we are left with only a '''general''' outline about Joseph.}}
# Not of a specific [[class]]; [[miscellaneous]]. {{defdate|from 16th c.}}
#: {{ux|en|[[general goods|'''general''' goods]]}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=2007|author=Alan Cheuse|title=A Little Death|journal=Southern Review|volume=43|number=3|page=692|passage=His measured, springless walk was the walk of the skilled countryman as distinct from the desultory shamble of the '''general''' labourer {{...}}.}}

